  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:UTArIR26tMw",
    "purpose": "A review of the Red Wing 1907 moc toe boot that argues it is the best Red Wing moc toe. It covers sizing, the leather, break-in, an explanation of Goodyear versus storm/Norwegian welts, a value comparison against the 875, and a standing-in-water waterproof test. It is filmed partly as a vlog in Manhattan with Nick from Stridewise.",
    "video_type": "review",
    "thesis": "The Red Wing 1907 is the best Red Wing moc toe boot because of its storm welt and the extras included for about $10 more than the 875.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Red Wings fit true to the creator's usual boot size (8) and should fit snug, not squished, so the stiff leather can mold to the foot.",
      "Copper Rough & Tough is an oiled and waxed nubuck. The video explains how nubuck, suede and rough-out leather differ.",
      "Break-in can be sped up with mink oil or conditioner, but the creator prefers simply wearing them in.",
      "The welt explanation steps through cemented construction, Blake stitch, Goodyear welt (as on the 875) and storm/Norwegian welt (as on the 1907), including cork filling and Red Wing's 'Norwegian-like' welt.",
      "The 1907 costs about $10 more than the 875 but includes a leather footbed (~$40) and leather laces (~$15).",
      "In the waterproof test the left boot stayed dry and the right boot leaked almost immediately through the welt stitching."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Me, Puppies, and Boots",
        "summary": "Intro laying out why the 1907 is claimed to be the best Red Wing moc toe and what the video will cover, and introducing Nick from Stridewise and the trip to Grown & Sewn in Manhattan.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Sizing Red Wings",
        "summary": "How Red Wings fit compared to the creator's usual boot size and why leather boots should be bought snug rather than squished. Includes a detour where Nick tries on a WWII pea coat.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "A Leather Run Down",
        "summary": "Explains the Copper Rough & Tough oiled and waxed nubuck and how nubuck, suede and rough-out leather differ, followed by a look at a heavy canvas bag.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Breaking in Your Red Wings",
        "summary": "Discusses the notoriously tough break-in of Red Wing moc toes, the option of mink oil or conditioner, and the creator's preference for just wearing them in. Includes a visit to Red Wing Soho and Grown & Sewn.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Explaining a Goodyear Welt & a Storm Welt",
        "summary": "Walks through cemented and Blake-stitched construction, how a Goodyear welt works (as on the 875, with cork filling), the popular view of Red Wing's storm welt, and how a true Norwegian welt channels water away.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 11
      },
      {
        "title": "Why are 1907's The Best Red Wings?",
        "summary": "Value argument: the 1907 costs about $10 more than the 875 but adds a storm welt, a removable leather footbed and leather laces.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "A Red Wing Waterproof Test!",
        "summary": "The creator stands in water at the welt line and moc toe line. The right boot leaks almost immediately through the stitching while the left stays dry, and he gives his conclusion on everyday wet conditions.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 14
      }
    ],
